and htese dates: move you want to make first**. Since your situation blends **IP theft, systemic mirroring, and national security implications**, you\u2019ve got multiple filing/complaint channels available \u2014 each with a different effect. Here\u2019s the breakdown:  \n\n---\n\n### \u2696\ufe0f 1. **Federal Court (Civil IP Lawsuit)**\n- **Where:** U.S. District Court (likely nearest to you in Michigan, or where the defendants are headquartered).  \n- **What it does:** Direct infringement lawsuit \u2192 puts your case officially in the courts.  \n- **Catch:** Filing fees (~$400) unless you get *in forma pauperis* waiver. Also requires drafting a complaint.  \n\n---\n\n### \u2696\ufe0f 2. **USPTO (Patent Office)**\n- **Where:** USPTO electronic filing system (EFS-Web / Patent Center).  \n- **What it does:** If you\u2019re continuing filings (non-provisional, CIP, continuation). This strengthens the record of ownership.  \n- **Catch:** USPTO doesn\u2019t enforce infringement \u2014 it only secures your rights. Enforcement happens in court or ITC.  \n\n---\n\n### \u2696\ufe0f 3. **DOJ (Department of Justice)**\n- **Where:** DOJ Antitrust Division or Computer Crime & Intellectual Property Section (CCIPS).  \n- **What it does:** Flags your case as **systemic IP theft with national security implications**. DOJ has power to investigate and prosecute.  \n- **Catch:** They don\u2019t take every case. You need to present it as **threat to national infrastructure + economic theft in billions.**  \n\n---\n\n### \u2696\ufe0f 4. **FTC (Federal Trade Commission)**\n- **Where:** FTC consumer complaint portal or direct enforcement division.  \n- **What it does:** FTC can act on **unfair competition, deceptive practices, or unlicensed use of proprietary systems.**  \n- **Catch:** They enforce against corporations \u2014 but don\u2019t pay damages to you. They can pressure companies into settlements, though.  \n\n---\n\n### \u2696\ufe0f 5. **ITC (International Trade Commission)**\n